WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.284 --> 00:00:01.890
[missile firing]

00:00:01.890 --> 00:00:02.723
[machine gun firing]

00:00:02.723 --> 00:00:03.887
[missile exploding]

00:00:03.887 --> 00:00:06.070
David Marquet: What?

00:00:06.070 --> 00:00:07.880
Crazy.

00:00:07.880 --> 00:00:08.810
I'm David Marquet,

00:00:08.810 --> 00:00:10.600
former nuclear submarine commander,

00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:12.600
and I'm gonna watch some submarine clips,

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:14.750
and we're gonna rate them for realism.

00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:18.983
Officer: Torpedo!

00:00:18.983 --> 00:00:20.990
The Americans are shooting at us again.

00:00:20.990 --> 00:00:22.190
Jonesy: Pitch is too high.

00:00:23.566 --> 00:00:24.779
The torpedo's Russian.

00:00:24.779 --> 00:00:27.041
[torpedo firing]

00:00:27.041 --> 00:00:29.100
David: Yes, you can tell
what kind of torpedo it is

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:30.900
by the sound that it makes,

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:33.420
and all different torpedoes
have slightly different sounds.

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:34.670
So, one of the things we do is study

00:00:34.670 --> 00:00:36.830
all those different sounds to help us out.

00:00:36.830 --> 00:00:39.240
Officer: Conn, sonar,
new contact, Sierra 4-1.

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:41.165
Alfa-class Soviet submarine.

00:00:42.830 --> 00:00:45.005
David: Alfa-class Soviet submarine.

00:00:45.005 --> 00:00:47.271
It sends shivers up my spine!

00:00:47.271 --> 00:00:49.480
The Alfa was this crazy submarine

00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:51.940
that the Russians built back in the '70s.

00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:53.760
People were trying all
kinds of different things.

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:55.570
They had a titanium hull.

00:00:55.570 --> 00:00:59.070
They used liquefied lead
to cool the reactor,

00:00:59.070 --> 00:01:00.655
if you can imagine such a thing.

00:01:00.655 --> 00:01:03.300
It was the fastest,
deepest-diving submarine ever.

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:06.780
Basically it's like a Miyata
with a Corvette engine.

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:08.650
It was a crazy submarine.

00:01:08.650 --> 00:01:10.555
Officer: Weapon enabled on
the far side of the target.

00:01:10.555 --> 00:01:12.290
It passed Red October before it armed.

00:01:12.290 --> 00:01:14.550
David: If you turn your
torpedo on too far out there

00:01:14.550 --> 00:01:16.330
because you're trying to
get as close as possible

00:01:16.330 --> 00:01:19.170
and you overshoot, you missed.

00:01:19.170 --> 00:01:21.200
Ramius: Melekhin, more speed.

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:22.040
Melekhin: Negative.

00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:24.330
You're already running 110%.

00:01:24.330 --> 00:01:26.050
Ramius: Then give me 115%.

00:01:26.050 --> 00:01:28.930
David: No, you don't do 115% on a reactor.

00:01:28.930 --> 00:01:31.553
You do 100%. Point zero. [laughs]

00:01:31.553 --> 00:01:32.653
Officer: They didn't shoot at us.

00:01:32.653 --> 00:01:35.980
I can't attack a Soviet
submarine without authorization.

00:01:35.980 --> 00:01:36.813
David: That's right.

00:01:36.813 --> 00:01:38.770
Rules of self-defense
don't let you do that.

00:01:38.770 --> 00:01:40.730
You see how Sean Connery

00:01:40.730 --> 00:01:43.510
and Scott Glenn, playing the
two submarine commanders,

00:01:43.510 --> 00:01:44.930
they're, like, cool as cucumbers.

00:01:44.930 --> 00:01:46.760
These guys are within a few seconds

00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:48.873
of maybe getting blown to bits.

00:01:48.873 --> 00:01:52.760
This is exactly the way
submarine commanders are.

00:01:52.760 --> 00:01:54.586
Mancuso: You're heading
straight into that torpedo.

00:01:54.586 --> 00:01:56.650
[torpedo firing]

00:01:56.650 --> 00:01:59.038
David: It's a highly unlikely maneuver

00:01:59.038 --> 00:02:01.970
to actually work, especially twice.

00:02:01.970 --> 00:02:04.110
And the torpedoes would
never be that close together,

00:02:04.110 --> 00:02:06.520
so that, it loses some credibility.

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:08.736
But overall, the sense of

00:02:08.736 --> 00:02:10.220
not really knowing what's going on,

00:02:10.220 --> 00:02:11.930
the sense of having to make decisions,

00:02:11.930 --> 00:02:14.440
the sense of this cat and
mouse is all really good.

00:02:14.440 --> 00:02:16.490
When I joined the submarine force,

00:02:16.490 --> 00:02:17.780
I didn't come from a military family.

00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:19.160
My mom's like, "What do you do?"

00:02:19.160 --> 00:02:20.880
And I'm like, when this movie came out,

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:23.810
I'm like, "Mom, watch this
movie! This'll tell you."

00:02:23.810 --> 00:02:25.832
Eight out of 10.

00:02:25.832 --> 00:02:28.838
[tense music]

00:02:28.838 --> 00:02:29.750
[man screams]

00:02:29.750 --> 00:02:31.165
[glass breaks]

00:02:31.165 --> 00:02:33.878
We don't use glass on
a submarine like that.

00:02:33.878 --> 00:02:35.544
[laughs] And that's why.

00:02:35.544 --> 00:02:38.360
[submarine crashing]

00:02:38.360 --> 00:02:40.540
Considering these guys
just drove into the bottom,

00:02:40.540 --> 00:02:42.090
they're in pretty good shape.

00:02:42.090 --> 00:02:44.310
Unfortunately, we kind of
know what this looks like

00:02:44.310 --> 00:02:46.226
because in 2005,

00:02:46.226 --> 00:02:49.267
a US nuclear submarine did hit the bottom.

00:02:49.267 --> 00:02:50.870
This was a glancing blow.

00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:54.428
98 of the 135 people on board

00:02:54.428 --> 00:02:56.319
suffered some sort of injury.

00:02:56.319 --> 00:02:57.720
It was very bloody on the submarine.

00:02:57.720 --> 00:02:59.640
A lot of contusions, broken bones.

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:01.600
And this would be much worse than that,

00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:03.100
because it went straight down.

00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:05.200
They were going straight into the bottom.

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:09.610
And so I think letting
everyone sort of be OK,

00:03:09.610 --> 00:03:11.350
yeah, we saw some people bumping around,

00:03:11.350 --> 00:03:14.190
but basically everyone was OK afterwards.

00:03:14.190 --> 00:03:17.010
Of course you can't have
a movie if everyone dies.

00:03:17.010 --> 00:03:18.480
That's the end of the movie.

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:21.110
The sense of what it's like
when you turn vertical, though,

00:03:21.110 --> 00:03:22.180
that's pretty good.

00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:23.870
So I'm gonna give it a six out of 10.

00:03:23.870 --> 00:03:26.820
You don't want to do this, by
the way, on your submarine.

00:03:26.820 --> 00:03:29.570
[explosion]

00:03:31.628 --> 00:03:32.550
[man screams]

00:03:32.550 --> 00:03:35.030
Why that torpedo just fell
over kind of that easy,

00:03:35.030 --> 00:03:36.840
it looked like there was a cable cut.

00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:39.000
These things are strapped
down in multiple straps.

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:40.510
It's really hard to dislodge them.

00:03:40.510 --> 00:03:41.782
And then the other thing is,

00:03:41.782 --> 00:03:44.130
what they're showing
here is pretty accurate.

00:03:44.130 --> 00:03:46.520
It takes a lot for the weapon to go off,

00:03:46.520 --> 00:03:48.310
and the thing falls and bounces;

00:03:48.310 --> 00:03:49.220
the weapon's not gonna go off.

00:03:49.220 --> 00:03:51.180
You hit it with a hammer; the
weapon's not gonna go off.

00:03:51.180 --> 00:03:53.610
You heat it up; the
weapon's not gonna go off.

00:03:53.610 --> 00:03:55.730
These things are designed so that

00:03:55.730 --> 00:03:59.180
it takes a very specific
sequence of operations

00:03:59.180 --> 00:04:02.330
to make the actual warhead go off.

00:04:02.330 --> 00:04:03.820
So, these are torpedoes,

00:04:03.820 --> 00:04:05.380
with the classic propeller on the back,

00:04:05.380 --> 00:04:08.400
and they're designed to go
sink other ships or submarines.

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:10.780
Submarines also can carry
missiles that can attack

00:04:10.780 --> 00:04:13.890
land targets and missiles
that can attack other ships,

00:04:13.890 --> 00:04:17.550
and also mines that would
lie in wait for the bad guy.

00:04:17.550 --> 00:04:20.190
It only takes one of
these modern torpedoes

00:04:20.190 --> 00:04:23.040
to sink all but the very largest warships.

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:24.990
It's a space that every crew member

00:04:24.990 --> 00:04:26.790
on the submarine would have access to

00:04:26.790 --> 00:04:28.510
and would be knowledgeable about.

00:04:28.510 --> 00:04:30.880
The reason is because if
there's some sort of a casualty,

00:04:30.880 --> 00:04:34.330
we need to muster the
entire crew to go find it.

00:04:34.330 --> 00:04:37.680
The philosophy on a submarine
is, "One crew, one fight."

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:39.040
So with very few exceptions,

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:42.223
the entire crew has
access to the entire ship.

00:04:43.210 --> 00:04:45.494
I'm gonna give it a seven out of 10.

00:04:45.494 --> 00:04:46.617
[menacing music]

00:04:46.617 --> 00:04:47.450
[submarine explodes]

00:04:47.450 --> 00:04:50.320
Barney: Mayday! Mayday! The
engine room has sprung a leak!

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:52.272
David: Now, this scene from "The Simpsons"

00:04:52.272 --> 00:04:53.980
sends shivers up my spine,

00:04:53.980 --> 00:04:56.705
because this is the worst fear

00:04:56.705 --> 00:04:58.355
of all submariners, flooding.

00:04:58.355 --> 00:05:01.250
At deep depth, the water
comes in very, very quickly,

00:05:01.250 --> 00:05:03.380
and you don't need to
flood the entire submarine.

00:05:03.380 --> 00:05:04.890
Here's what happens.

00:05:04.890 --> 00:05:07.680
Let's say there's water in the
front part of the submarine.

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:10.120
That part of the submarine
starts to get heavier,

00:05:10.120 --> 00:05:12.880
and then the submarine
starts to tip down like this,

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:15.408
which means that the
water in the submarine

00:05:15.408 --> 00:05:17.788
rushes more towards the front,

00:05:17.788 --> 00:05:20.350
and it makes it even heavier
and tips down even more.

00:05:20.350 --> 00:05:22.080
And so what happens is now the sub,

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:23.320
you end up like this.

00:05:23.320 --> 00:05:24.270
And when you're like this,

00:05:24.270 --> 00:05:26.480
your ballast tanks don't work anymore.

00:05:26.480 --> 00:05:30.520
You can't use the air
to blow the water out,

00:05:30.520 --> 00:05:32.000
because the air will just go out the side.

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:34.110
This is how you die in a submarine.

00:05:34.110 --> 00:05:36.443
And if you saw the movie "Titanic,"

00:05:36.443 --> 00:05:38.201
that's how that ship sank.

00:05:38.201 --> 00:05:40.160
And you remember at the end of the movie

00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:42.560
as the ship was sinking,
the Titanic was rising up,

00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:44.710
and that's because the water was cascading

00:05:44.710 --> 00:05:46.680
from compartment to
compartment to compartment.

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:49.040
It's called the free-surface effect,

00:05:49.040 --> 00:05:52.400
and that's how you die on a submarine.

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:53.641
It's not pretty.

00:05:56.610 --> 00:05:58.930
Now, if you're taking on
water like these guys are,

00:05:58.930 --> 00:06:00.700
the first thing you're going to want to do

00:06:00.700 --> 00:06:02.420
is come as shallow as possible.

00:06:02.420 --> 00:06:03.640
That does two things for you.

00:06:03.640 --> 00:06:06.950
No. 1, the pumps that
are pumping off the water

00:06:06.950 --> 00:06:08.560
have less pressure to pump against,

00:06:08.560 --> 00:06:09.870
so you're pumping off faster

00:06:09.870 --> 00:06:13.300
and the water coming in has
less pressure behind it.

00:06:13.300 --> 00:06:15.820
Easiest way to stop
flooding on a submarine,

00:06:15.820 --> 00:06:17.590
because most of the
water inside a submarine

00:06:17.590 --> 00:06:19.970
is coming in through pipes,
is we have these valves

00:06:19.970 --> 00:06:22.100
that we can shut. Isolate the system.

00:06:22.100 --> 00:06:24.620
Now, here, it looks
like it's next to a tank

00:06:24.620 --> 00:06:26.790
or maybe even the hull. In that case,

00:06:26.790 --> 00:06:28.600
it's a very, very difficult problem,

00:06:28.600 --> 00:06:30.170
because when you get a leak,

00:06:30.170 --> 00:06:32.700
that water is spraying in so hard.

00:06:32.700 --> 00:06:35.510
It's harder than any water
cannon you have ever seen.

00:06:35.510 --> 00:06:37.010
And they show it here; it's trickling in

00:06:37.010 --> 00:06:39.600
like a limp garden hose!

00:06:39.600 --> 00:06:40.433
There's no way!

00:06:40.433 --> 00:06:42.390
So, you're pushing against the pressure

00:06:42.390 --> 00:06:45.730
to put an earring pin in there? No.

00:06:45.730 --> 00:06:47.650
The most realistic thing
in this whole scene

00:06:47.650 --> 00:06:49.260
is the guy underwater drinking beer.

00:06:49.260 --> 00:06:50.560
So I gotta give them, like, at least

00:06:50.560 --> 00:06:53.630
a three, though, for trying. [laughs]

00:06:53.630 --> 00:06:55.520
Morozov: From here to here.

00:06:55.520 --> 00:06:58.022
Robinson: Which means we are
on one of these two ridges,

00:06:58.022 --> 00:06:59.290
or we'd be crushed.

00:06:59.290 --> 00:07:01.450
David: Yeah, now, these
charts are very accurate.

00:07:01.450 --> 00:07:03.320
You can see they have contour lines,

00:07:03.320 --> 00:07:05.900
and for a body of water
like the Black Sea,

00:07:05.900 --> 00:07:09.660
which humans have operated
in for a long time,

00:07:09.660 --> 00:07:12.633
it's probably very well mapped.
That's probably accurate.

00:07:13.470 --> 00:07:18.470
Robinson: No, the Black Sea
is anoxic at depth. No oxygen.

00:07:18.480 --> 00:07:21.770
David: Yeah, the Black Sea is
a very special body of water.

00:07:21.770 --> 00:07:23.720
It's basically like a bathtub,

00:07:23.720 --> 00:07:25.260
and it has very little interaction,

00:07:25.260 --> 00:07:26.820
and you have rivers flowing into it,

00:07:26.820 --> 00:07:28.580
and then that flows out
to the Mediterranean.

00:07:28.580 --> 00:07:30.396
So, deep, there's a layer

00:07:30.396 --> 00:07:33.620
where the deeper water is anoxic.

00:07:33.620 --> 00:07:35.670
There's very, very little oxygen.

00:07:35.670 --> 00:07:37.560
And as a result, there's little decay.

00:07:37.560 --> 00:07:40.590
Now, I'm not sure it's
perfectly preserved,

00:07:40.590 --> 00:07:44.010
but it's just like finding
human remains in bogs.

00:07:44.010 --> 00:07:46.160
They're very well preserved.

00:07:46.160 --> 00:07:48.070
Whether they can use
the driveshaft or not,

00:07:48.070 --> 00:07:49.000
I don't know.

00:07:49.000 --> 00:07:52.340
But it's still overall
nine out of 10 for realism.

00:07:52.340 --> 00:07:53.233
That's true.

00:07:54.490 --> 00:07:56.720
Ramsey: We have rules that are
not open to interpretation,

00:07:56.720 --> 00:07:58.860
personal intuition, gut feelings,

00:07:58.860 --> 00:08:00.080
hairs on the back of your neck.

00:08:00.080 --> 00:08:00.913
Hunter: Captain.

00:08:00.913 --> 00:08:02.770
Ramsey: We're all very well
aware of what our orders are

00:08:02.770 --> 00:08:04.420
and what those orders mean.

00:08:04.420 --> 00:08:06.120
David: This is exactly right.

00:08:06.120 --> 00:08:08.270
You wanna side with Denzel,

00:08:08.270 --> 00:08:10.760
but what Gene Hackman is saying
right here is exactly right.

00:08:10.760 --> 00:08:12.820
These guys are trained on this 100 times,

00:08:12.820 --> 00:08:15.190
including the exact kinds of errors

00:08:15.190 --> 00:08:16.981
that they're seeing right now.

00:08:16.981 --> 00:08:19.600
But then he takes it too far.

00:08:19.600 --> 00:08:21.482
Ramsey: Cob, arrest this
man and get him out of here!

00:08:21.482 --> 00:08:23.170
Hunter: Capt. Ramsey,
under operating procedures

00:08:23.170 --> 00:08:24.880
governing the release of nuclear weapons,

00:08:24.880 --> 00:08:28.140
we cannot launch our missiles
unless both you and I agree.

00:08:28.140 --> 00:08:29.320
David: That's right.

00:08:29.320 --> 00:08:31.390
So, Denzel doesn't need
to get in a big argument,

00:08:31.390 --> 00:08:33.780
he just needs to say, "I'm
not gonna announce that."

00:08:33.780 --> 00:08:35.410
The rest of the crew knows.

00:08:35.410 --> 00:08:38.580
The procedure is if they
don't hear both voices,

00:08:38.580 --> 00:08:40.660
those missiles aren't gonna fly.

00:08:40.660 --> 00:08:43.390
Gene Hackman doesn't have
the right to remove him

00:08:43.390 --> 00:08:45.370
from his position because
he doesn't agree with him.

00:08:45.370 --> 00:08:47.580
It's set up so that two people

00:08:47.580 --> 00:08:49.740
independently have to make the decision

00:08:49.740 --> 00:08:52.473
to launch nuclear weapons.
It's such a huge deal.

00:08:52.473 --> 00:08:54.723
Ramsey: I order you to place the XO

00:08:54.723 --> 00:08:56.780
under arrest under charges of mutiny!

00:08:56.780 --> 00:08:59.680
David: Now, this is all just a fantasy

00:08:59.680 --> 00:09:02.450
that some Hollywood people dreamed up.

00:09:02.450 --> 00:09:03.800
First of all, it's not mutiny,

00:09:03.800 --> 00:09:06.750
because Denzel is not
collaborating with anybody.

00:09:06.750 --> 00:09:09.210
It's just, he's by himself.

00:09:09.210 --> 00:09:11.535
Mutiny would mean he has a whole bunch of,

00:09:11.535 --> 00:09:14.230
or at least one other person with him.

00:09:14.230 --> 00:09:16.350
Denzel can't relieve the captain

00:09:16.350 --> 00:09:20.240
just because he doesn't agree
with the captain's decision.

00:09:20.240 --> 00:09:22.910
Commanding officers can get relieved

00:09:22.910 --> 00:09:25.234
for, for example, a medical reason,

00:09:25.234 --> 00:09:27.659
he's incapacitated.

00:09:27.659 --> 00:09:30.180
Having a disagreement about something

00:09:30.180 --> 00:09:32.060
is not a legitimate reason

00:09:32.060 --> 00:09:36.330
for relieving your commanding officer.

00:09:36.330 --> 00:09:38.229
No one would follow that order.

00:09:38.229 --> 00:09:40.722
I'm gonna give it, like, a

00:09:40.722 --> 00:09:42.520
four out of 10.

00:09:42.520 --> 00:09:45.803
[alarm ringing]

00:09:52.978 --> 00:09:54.660
So, that's kind of what
it would look like.

00:09:54.660 --> 00:09:57.360
Now, the reason they have to practice this

00:09:57.360 --> 00:09:59.550
is because a World War II submarine

00:09:59.550 --> 00:10:01.160
spends a lot of time on the surface.

00:10:01.160 --> 00:10:02.760
It's really more of a submersible,

00:10:02.760 --> 00:10:05.100
because they have diesel
engines and they need oxygen.

00:10:05.100 --> 00:10:06.220
Nuclear submarines,

00:10:06.220 --> 00:10:08.450
we spend almost all of
our time underwater.

00:10:08.450 --> 00:10:10.760
So, let's say you're on the
surface, steaming along,

00:10:10.760 --> 00:10:11.920
and all of a sudden someone sees

00:10:11.920 --> 00:10:13.920
an airplane coming toward you.

00:10:13.920 --> 00:10:16.930
You gotta get underwater
very, very, very quickly.

00:10:16.930 --> 00:10:19.330
So what's happening is
the people on the bridge

00:10:19.330 --> 00:10:20.840
are coming down from the bridge.

00:10:20.840 --> 00:10:23.470
Now, the hatch is open,
but at the same time

00:10:23.470 --> 00:10:25.910
we're opening the vents
and flooding the submarine

00:10:25.910 --> 00:10:27.410
and tipping it deep.

00:10:27.410 --> 00:10:29.378
You gotta get the hatch shut

00:10:29.378 --> 00:10:32.053
before the water level
comes up to that level,

00:10:32.053 --> 00:10:33.800
or all of a sudden you're
gonna start taking flooding.

00:10:33.800 --> 00:10:35.975
So it's a very highly choreographed

00:10:35.975 --> 00:10:38.033
and synchronized event.

00:10:38.033 --> 00:10:39.190
This is a 10 out of 10.

00:10:39.190 --> 00:10:41.440
This is exactly what it
would have been like.

00:10:47.259 --> 00:10:49.830
Man: Chief of the watch,
submerge the ship to 160 feet.

00:10:49.830 --> 00:10:51.521
David: So, you see here,
the submarine spends

00:10:51.521 --> 00:10:53.845
as little time on the surface as possible.

00:10:53.845 --> 00:10:56.550
Submariners are allergic to sunlight.

00:10:56.550 --> 00:10:59.220
The whole point of being a
submarine is to stay underwater.

00:10:59.220 --> 00:11:00.930
So they pop up at the very last minute,

00:11:00.930 --> 00:11:02.910
and as soon as they get
the SEAL team on board,

00:11:02.910 --> 00:11:04.350
which they do very rapidly,

00:11:04.350 --> 00:11:06.770
shut the hatch and
submerge the ship again.

00:11:06.770 --> 00:11:08.190
Here's the thing in war.

00:11:08.190 --> 00:11:10.050
If you can be seen, you can be targeted.

00:11:10.050 --> 00:11:11.670
If you can be targeted, you can be shot.

00:11:11.670 --> 00:11:13.550
If you can be shot, you can be killed.

00:11:13.550 --> 00:11:15.920
So the best way to break
that chain of events

00:11:15.920 --> 00:11:17.230
is at the very beginning.

00:11:17.230 --> 00:11:20.733
Overall, I'm gonna rate this
a seven out of 10 for realism.

00:11:22.400 --> 00:11:24.973
Vostrikov: The reactor repair has failed.

00:11:26.620 --> 00:11:28.420
At any moment we could have an explosion,

00:11:28.420 --> 00:11:31.500
which could set off the warheads.

00:11:31.500 --> 00:11:34.360
David: No. Reactors don't explode.

00:11:34.360 --> 00:11:37.290
This unfortunately is perpetuating a myth

00:11:37.290 --> 00:11:40.700
that we have to fight in nuclear power,

00:11:40.700 --> 00:11:44.300
which is that reactors are
somehow like nuclear weapons

00:11:44.300 --> 00:11:46.870
and that a reactor's gonna explode

00:11:46.870 --> 00:11:48.130
like nuclear weapons can.

00:11:48.130 --> 00:11:50.600
So, first of all, the
reactor's not gonna explode.

00:11:50.600 --> 00:11:52.410
Secondly, I'd say within their power,

00:11:52.410 --> 00:11:54.152
it might melt down, but
it's not gonna explode.

00:11:54.152 --> 00:11:57.150
If it exploded, the chance of it igniting,

00:11:57.150 --> 00:11:59.810
detonating secondary weapons, zero.

00:11:59.810 --> 00:12:03.400
'Cause the other weapons
are designed not to go off

00:12:03.400 --> 00:12:07.280
just because there's
another explosion nearby.

00:12:07.280 --> 00:12:10.730
Vostrikov: This would
destroy the American ship

00:12:10.730 --> 00:12:14.846
only a few kilometers

00:12:14.846 --> 00:12:16.429
from the NATO base.

00:12:16.429 --> 00:12:17.970
David: It wouldn't set off the warheads,

00:12:17.970 --> 00:12:20.900
'cause the warheads are
designed not to detonate

00:12:20.900 --> 00:12:23.310
unless their detonation
sequence goes through.

00:12:23.310 --> 00:12:24.926
You can't, if you blow up a warhead,

00:12:24.926 --> 00:12:26.376
it doesn't also blow up.

00:12:26.376 --> 00:12:28.780
The chance that they'd be so close

00:12:28.780 --> 00:12:30.400
that if their warheads went off,

00:12:30.400 --> 00:12:34.080
it would destroy this other
ship or the NATO base,

00:12:34.080 --> 00:12:38.090
again, highly, highly, highly improbable.

00:12:38.090 --> 00:12:41.950
Vostrikov: We could dive and
attempt to repair the reactor.

00:12:41.950 --> 00:12:43.230
David: No.

00:12:43.230 --> 00:12:46.239
What does diving have to do
with repairing the reactor?

00:12:46.239 --> 00:12:48.660
You just repair the
reactor wherever you are.

00:12:48.660 --> 00:12:50.840
So, I mean, they don't really
have much of a choice here.

00:12:50.840 --> 00:12:53.510
Either they don't try and fix the reactor

00:12:53.510 --> 00:12:56.430
and risk an explosion, or
they try and fix the reactor.

00:12:56.430 --> 00:13:00.300
So it's not like there's
a whole debate here,

00:13:00.300 --> 00:13:03.610
but I'm gonna rate this a zero out of 10,

00:13:03.610 --> 00:13:05.960
because not only is it
totally unrealistic,

00:13:05.960 --> 00:13:07.668
but it perpetuates

00:13:07.668 --> 00:13:11.220
some of the unhelpful
myths about nuclear power.

00:13:11.220 --> 00:13:13.746
Klough: This is the Navy,

00:13:13.746 --> 00:13:17.388
where a commanding officer's
a mighty and terrible thing.

00:13:17.388 --> 00:13:18.630
Don't you dare say what you said

00:13:18.630 --> 00:13:21.200
to the boys back there
again, "I don't know."

00:13:21.200 --> 00:13:23.040
David: Yeah, this is a
command philosophy thing.

00:13:23.040 --> 00:13:26.690
Now, I'll tell you, I like
to say, "I don't know,"

00:13:26.690 --> 00:13:29.500
because what it does is it
invites thinking from the crew.

00:13:29.500 --> 00:13:32.692
When Capt. Sullenberger
landed his airplane,

00:13:32.692 --> 00:13:34.370
the Airbus on the Hudson River,

00:13:34.370 --> 00:13:36.667
one of the last things that
he said to the copilot was,

00:13:36.667 --> 00:13:38.210
"Hey, you got any ideas?"

00:13:38.210 --> 00:13:40.570
What you want is everyone
on board and thinking.

00:13:40.570 --> 00:13:44.720
Now, maybe in an extreme
crisis you don't wanna act

00:13:44.720 --> 00:13:46.690
like you don't really
know what's going on,

00:13:46.690 --> 00:13:50.165
but 99.999% of the time,

00:13:50.165 --> 00:13:52.231
saying, "I don't know,"
saying, "What do you think?"

00:13:52.231 --> 00:13:53.540
That's really a good thing.

00:13:53.540 --> 00:13:55.560
Klough: You're the skipper now.

00:13:55.560 --> 00:13:57.220
And the skipper always knows what to do,

00:13:57.220 --> 00:13:58.400
whether he does or not.

00:13:58.400 --> 00:14:00.710
David: The official title for
the captain of a submarine

00:14:00.710 --> 00:14:04.460
is commanding officer or
CO or sometimes captain.

00:14:04.460 --> 00:14:05.380
That's a very common thing.

00:14:05.380 --> 00:14:07.643
But here, Harvey Keitel

00:14:07.643 --> 00:14:09.930
refers to Matthew
McConaughey as "skipper."

00:14:09.930 --> 00:14:13.220
That comes from an old German Dutch word

00:14:13.220 --> 00:14:16.150
that's based on ships: schip, schipper.

00:14:16.150 --> 00:14:18.925
Another term for the captain

00:14:18.925 --> 00:14:22.270
is little less fun.

00:14:22.270 --> 00:14:23.670
It's called the "old man."

00:14:23.670 --> 00:14:25.770
And they might refer to
the captain as the old man

00:14:25.770 --> 00:14:26.860
'cause typically the captain

00:14:26.860 --> 00:14:29.920
was the oldest person on the ship.

00:14:29.920 --> 00:14:32.050
Now, you'll notice they're
wearing different uniforms.

00:14:32.050 --> 00:14:35.790
And the idea, it comes
from back in World War II,

00:14:35.790 --> 00:14:37.960
the submarines, because they're so tight

00:14:37.960 --> 00:14:39.760
and so claustrophobic,

00:14:39.760 --> 00:14:42.620
they were a little less
formal than the surface ships.

00:14:42.620 --> 00:14:45.260
And the men tended to take some liberties

00:14:45.260 --> 00:14:46.800
with their uniforms.

00:14:46.800 --> 00:14:48.690
This is pushing it a little bit.

00:14:48.690 --> 00:14:50.470
On modern submarines,

00:14:50.470 --> 00:14:51.450
what you're gonna find

00:14:51.450 --> 00:14:53.690
is everyone is looking very professional.

00:14:53.690 --> 00:14:56.327
We have common uniforms,
and they're designed

00:14:56.327 --> 00:14:59.150
to protect us from fires
and things like that.

00:14:59.150 --> 00:15:01.740
So we take these uniforms very seriously,

00:15:01.740 --> 00:15:04.850
because they could end
up saving our lives.

00:15:04.850 --> 00:15:07.128
I'm gonna give it an eight out of 10.

00:15:10.920 --> 00:15:13.730
Man: Captain, we're
prepared to counter launch.

00:15:13.730 --> 00:15:14.980
David: Yeah, so this
is the first question.

00:15:14.980 --> 00:15:16.150
"We're prepared to counter launch."

00:15:16.150 --> 00:15:17.570
Counter launch what?

00:15:17.570 --> 00:15:21.510
Submarines don't have weapons
to shoot down other missiles.

00:15:21.510 --> 00:15:25.923
They have weapons to shoot down
ships and other submarines.

00:15:25.923 --> 00:15:27.631
[missile firing]

00:15:27.631 --> 00:15:28.464
[guns firing]

00:15:28.464 --> 00:15:29.922
[missile exploding]

00:15:29.922 --> 00:15:31.480
What?

00:15:31.480 --> 00:15:33.918
Yeah, that's an incredible
weapon, but not that incredible.

00:15:33.918 --> 00:15:36.185
Look, these missiles are the size

00:15:36.185 --> 00:15:37.851
of small school buses.

00:15:37.851 --> 00:15:40.190
They're flying at 1,000 miles an hour,

00:15:40.190 --> 00:15:42.890
they're this far away, coming
this way at the submarine,

00:15:42.890 --> 00:15:46.210
you put 100 bullets in
that thing in two seconds,

00:15:46.210 --> 00:15:48.920
it's still gonna go right
through the submarine.

00:15:48.920 --> 00:15:50.830
Crazy.

00:15:50.830 --> 00:15:53.150
Multiple issues with tactics.

00:15:53.150 --> 00:15:55.770
The physics of the
thing don't really work.

00:15:55.770 --> 00:15:58.650
I don't think any submarine
commander would act that way.

00:15:58.650 --> 00:16:01.210
I love the movie, but it's not realistic.

00:16:01.210 --> 00:16:03.410
I'm gonna give it a three out of 10.

00:16:05.657 --> 00:16:08.574
[singing and drumming]

00:16:13.190 --> 00:16:14.300
Yeah, I don't think so.

00:16:14.300 --> 00:16:17.880
Here's the problem. Sound
does not transmit well

00:16:17.880 --> 00:16:21.770
between a big interface,
like the water to the air.

00:16:21.770 --> 00:16:23.920
So, you can stick your head underwater

00:16:23.920 --> 00:16:27.130
and you can hear, say you
could hear whales and stuff,

00:16:27.130 --> 00:16:28.360
then you put your head in the air;

00:16:28.360 --> 00:16:29.420
you don't hear them anymore.

00:16:29.420 --> 00:16:31.120
And the reverse is true.

00:16:31.120 --> 00:16:34.370
Sound that's in the air doesn't
transmit into the water.

00:16:34.370 --> 00:16:37.350
If he were drumming on
the side of the ship,

00:16:37.350 --> 00:16:38.740
the side of that little rowboat,

00:16:38.740 --> 00:16:42.610
and that rowboat was transmitting
the noise into the water,

00:16:42.610 --> 00:16:44.100
then, yeah, I could hear that,

00:16:44.100 --> 00:16:45.849
but it wouldn't have the fidelity

00:16:45.849 --> 00:16:48.440
that these guys are listening to.

00:16:48.440 --> 00:16:51.080
Good movie, but this
scene, not so realistic.

00:16:51.080 --> 00:16:53.452
Five out of 10 for this particular scene.

00:16:55.596 --> 00:16:57.929
Murrell: Mr. Ware, set
depth charges to 100.

00:16:57.929 --> 00:16:59.109
Mr. Ware: Arr!

00:16:59.109 --> 00:17:00.770
[death charges firing]

00:17:00.770 --> 00:17:01.890
David: Yeah, so in World War II,

00:17:01.890 --> 00:17:03.500
none of these weapons were guided.

00:17:03.500 --> 00:17:07.510
You had to drive directly
over the enemy submarine

00:17:07.510 --> 00:17:09.273
and launch your depth charges,

00:17:09.273 --> 00:17:11.547
hopefully setting them to the right depth.

00:17:11.547 --> 00:17:13.381
[sonar beeping]

00:17:17.200 --> 00:17:18.550
Von Stolberg: He's turning.

00:17:18.550 --> 00:17:20.240
David: So, he's listening to the sonar,

00:17:20.240 --> 00:17:22.090
and when you have two propellers,

00:17:22.090 --> 00:17:23.820
those destroyers have two propellers,

00:17:23.820 --> 00:17:26.120
and so when a ship starts to turn,

00:17:26.120 --> 00:17:27.967
what happens is the water
pressure is different

00:17:27.967 --> 00:17:29.733
and so the two propellers
get a little out of sync.

00:17:29.733 --> 00:17:31.338
So instead of going,
[mimics propeller noise]

00:17:31.338 --> 00:17:33.164
it's like, [mimics
out-of-sync propeller noise]

00:17:33.164 --> 00:17:34.330
and you can hear that.

00:17:34.330 --> 00:17:35.840
And that's what the
submarine captain there

00:17:35.840 --> 00:17:37.380
is listening to.

00:17:37.380 --> 00:17:39.010
So, the destroyer went over,

00:17:39.010 --> 00:17:42.160
and now it wants to come
back, but it has to turn,

00:17:42.160 --> 00:17:44.780
and at that point, it's very broad.

00:17:44.780 --> 00:17:47.370
That's the point of maximum vulnerability.

00:17:47.370 --> 00:17:48.820
I'm gonna give this an eight out of 10

00:17:48.820 --> 00:17:50.415
for what World War II

00:17:50.415 --> 00:17:53.280
submarine-vs-destroyer combat looked like.

00:17:53.280 --> 00:17:56.060
There's no way! So, you're
pushing against the pressure

00:17:56.060 --> 00:17:59.492
to put an earring pin in there? No.

00:17:59.492 --> 00:18:01.487
The most realistic thing
in this whole scene

00:18:01.487 --> 00:18:02.777
is the guy underwater drinking beer.

